// 012 — Print command-line arguments — DESIGN NOTE
// `argc` and `argv` are passed by the kernel at exec time, accessible
// in C as `int main(int argc, char **argv)`. Every language wraps
// this in some shape — Python `sys.argv`, Go `os.Args`, Rust
// `env::args()`.
//
// AiLang's `_start` entry already receives this information (the
// SysV ABI puts argc + argv on the stack), and the compiler itself
// uses it (look at how `ailang_cli.ailang` parses its own argv).
// What's missing is a small library wrapper that hands the calling
// program an indexable view, like:
//
//     Args_Count()         -> Integer
//     Args_Get(idx)        -> Address
//
// Until that wrapper lands in `Librarys/OS/Linux/Library.OS.Args`,
// programs that need argv must either reach for the raw stack
// pointer (compiler-internal pattern) or wait.
SubRoutine.Main {
    PrintMessage("(planned: Args_Count() and Args_Get(i) under Library.OS.Args)\n")
    PrintMessage("until then, see ailang_cli.ailang for the raw-stack pattern\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
